Setup: a future adult group session with free places + 2 adult swimmers
====================================================================
Session 29225 ( Group Adult , capacity 4 , starts 2026-11-12 06:03 ) — swimmers 4 and 7

====================================================================
PATH A — SUCCESS: enrol + valid payment, then COMMIT
====================================================================
 enrolments_before | payments_before 
-------------------+-----------------
                 1 |               1
(1 row)

BEGIN
 new_enrolment_id 
------------------
            60014
(1 row)

COMMIT
 enrolments_after | payments_after 
------------------+----------------
                2 |              2
(1 row)

  ^ both +1: the enrolment AND its payment were committed together.

====================================================================
PATH B1 — FAILURE INSIDE THE FUNCTION: invalid payment method
  The enrolment row is inserted first; then the payment INSERT breaks the
  payment_method CHECK. The whole call fails, so the enrolment disappears too.
====================================================================
BEGIN
psql:commit_rollback_demo.sql:53: ERROR:  Payment failed for enrolment 60015: new row for relation "payment" violates check constraint "payment_payment_method_check"
CONTEXT:  PL/pgSQL function enrol_swimmer(integer,integer,text) line 64 at RAISE
ROLLBACK
 sw2_enrolments | sw2_payments 
----------------+--------------
              0 |            0
(1 row)

  ^ both 0: no half-finished booking was left behind (atomicity).

====================================================================
PATH B2 — EXPLICIT ROLLBACK: enrol succeeds, then the card is declined
====================================================================
BEGIN
 new_enrolment_id 
------------------
            60016
(1 row)

Inside the open transaction swimmer 7 is enrolled:
 sw2_enrolments_inside_tx 
--------------------------
                        1
(1 row)

Payment gateway says DECLINED -> the application issues ROLLBACK
ROLLBACK
 sw2_enrolments_after_rollback | sw2_payments_after_rollback 
-------------------------------+-----------------------------
                             0 |                           0
(1 row)


CONCLUSION: Path A persisted both rows; Paths B1 and B2 left nothing behind.

==> [3/5] Isolation levels demo (READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ vs SERIALIZABLE)
Pager usage is off.
====================================================================
PHENOMENON 1: NON-REPEATABLE READ
  Under READ COMMITTED, the same SELECT inside one transaction can
  return different rows if another transaction commits a change in
  between. Under REPEATABLE READ, the SELECT returns the same snapshot.
====================================================================

Target swimmer BEFORE:
 swimmer_id | first_name 
------------+------------
          3 | Emeka
(1 row)


--- Under READ COMMITTED (default) ---
T1 starts a transaction and reads the swimmer. THEN a second psql
session updates the swimmer and commits. T1 reads again — under
READ COMMITTED the new value IS visible (NON-REPEATABLE READ).
BEGIN
T1 first read:
 first_name 
------------
 Emeka
(1 row)

  [second session runs UPDATE + COMMIT in background]
UPDATE 1
T1 second read (same transaction):
   first_name    
-----------------
 TEMP_RC_RENAMED
(1 row)

  ^^^ Should show TEMP_RC_RENAMED — NON-REPEATABLE READ happened.
ROLLBACK
UPDATE 1

--- Under REPEATABLE READ ---
T1 starts a REPEATABLE READ transaction; both reads see the SAME snapshot.
BEGIN
T1 first read:
 first_name 
------------
 Emeka
(1 row)

UPDATE 1
T1 second read (same REPEATABLE READ transaction):
 first_name 
------------
 Emeka
(1 row)

  ^^^ Should show the ORIGINAL name — snapshot is frozen, no non-repeatable read.
ROLLBACK
UPDATE 1

====================================================================
PHENOMENON 2: PHANTOM READ
  Under READ COMMITTED, a COUNT(*) can change between two reads in
  the same transaction if another transaction inserts/deletes matching
  rows. PostgreSQL REPEATABLE READ prevents phantom reads.
====================================================================

--- Under READ COMMITTED ---
BEGIN
T1 first count of Adult swimmers:
 adults_before 
---------------
          5865
(1 row)

INSERT 0 1
T1 second count (same transaction):
 adults_after 
--------------
         5866
(1 row)

  ^^^ adults_after should be adults_before + 1 — PHANTOM READ happened.
ROLLBACK
DELETE 1

--- Under REPEATABLE READ ---
BEGIN
T1 first count of Adult swimmers:
 adults_before 
---------------
          5865
(1 row)

INSERT 0 1
T1 second count (same REPEATABLE READ transaction):
 adults_after 
--------------
         5865
(1 row)

  ^^^ adults_after should EQUAL adults_before — PHANTOM READ prevented.
ROLLBACK
DELETE 1

====================================================================
PHENOMENON 3: WRITE SKEW (only prevented by SERIALIZABLE)
  Under REPEATABLE READ, write skew is POSSIBLE.
  Under SERIALIZABLE, PostgreSQL SSI detects the conflict and aborts.
  run_race_demos.py Part 4 shows SSI aborting a booking, then the retry.
====================================================================

Quick demonstration of SERIALIZABLE aborting on conflict:
T1 starts a SERIALIZABLE transaction and reads swimmer count.
T2 starts SERIALIZABLE, reads same count, INSERTs, COMMITs.
T1 then INSERTs and COMMITs — should get could not serialize access.
BEGIN
 t1_count 
----------
     5865
(1 row)

BEGIN
 count 
-------
  5865
(1 row)

INSERT 0 1
COMMIT
T1 now INSERTs and COMMITs (should fail with serialization conflict):
psql:isolation_levels.sql:139: ERROR:  could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
DETAIL:  Reason code: Canceled on identification as a pivot, during write.
HINT:  The transaction might succeed if retried.
ROLLBACK
  ^^^ T1 should fail with: ERROR: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
      (the INSERT or the COMMIT reports it; a failed COMMIT ends the transaction as a ROLLBACK)
DELETE 1

====================================================================
ISOLATION LEVELS SUMMARY
====================================================================
READ COMMITTED  (PG default): no dirty reads; non-repeatable reads POSSIBLE; phantoms POSSIBLE; write skew POSSIBLE.
REPEATABLE READ (PG):          no dirty reads; no non-repeatable reads; NO phantoms (PG-specific); write skew STILL POSSIBLE.
SERIALIZABLE  (PG SSI):        no dirty reads; no non-repeatable reads; no phantoms; no write skew. Conflicts abort with retry hint.

