Pager usage is off.
===== DEADLOCK DEMO =====
Lock targets: swimmer 3 and swimmer 4
Launching T_A and T_B at the same time (each in its own psql session)...
----- T_A output -----
T_A: lock s1
BEGIN
UPDATE 1
 pg_sleep 
----------
 
(1 row)

T_A: now lock s2
psql:/tmp/splash_ta.sql:6: ERROR:  deadlock detected
DETAIL:  Process 28976 waits for ShareLock on transaction 869; blocked by process 28975.
Process 28975 waits for ShareLock on transaction 868; blocked by process 28976.
HINT:  See server log for query details.
CONTEXT:  while locking tuple (191,9) in relation "swimmer"
ROLLBACK
----- T_B output -----
T_B: lock s2
BEGIN
UPDATE 1
 pg_sleep 
----------
 
(1 row)

T_B: now lock s1
UPDATE 1
COMMIT

One session shows ERROR: deadlock detected (it was rolled back); the other COMMITs.
Fix: always lock rows in the SAME order (e.g. ascending swimmer_id) — see run_race_demos.py Part 6.

==> [5/5] Race-condition demos, 6 parts, two real sessions -> race_demos_output.log

==============================================================================
SETUP — a demo Group Children session (capacity 6) 21 days ahead
==============================================================================
Demo session_id = 29763 (pool 1, lane 1, 2026-12-02 18:00-18:45 Lagos), capacity = 6
Swimmers used: 1=Musiliu Akpan, 2=Tunde Bashir, 6=Rukayat Ibrahim, 9=Wale Okafor, 14=Bukola Garba, 16=Jide Eze, 17=Amina Toko, 18=Femi Adebayo

==============================================================================
PART 1 — THE BUG: two parents book the LAST place at the same time (READ COMMITTED)
==============================================================================
[reset] session 29763 now has 5/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (5,)
[B] BEGIN
[B]   -> BEGIN
[B] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[B]   -> (5,)
Both sessions see 5 of 6 -> both think one place is free.
[A] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[A]   -> INSERT 0 1
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 18, 'confirmed')
[B]   -> INSERT 0 1
(trg_group_capacity also counted only COMMITTED rows, so it let both through)
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
[B] COMMIT
[B]   -> COMMIT
[check] enrolled in session 29763: 7/6
RESULT: OVERBOOKED — 7 swimmers in a 6-place lesson.

==============================================================================
PART 2 — UNIQUE (session_id, swimmer_id): the same child booked twice at once
==============================================================================
[reset] session 29763 now has 2/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[A]   -> INSERT 0 1
[B] BEGIN
[B]   -> BEGIN
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[B]   ... BLOCKED (pg_stat_activity.wait_event_type = Lock)
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
[B]   -> ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint "enrolment_session_id_swimmer_id_key"   (SQLSTATE 23505)
[B] ROLLBACK
[B]   -> ROLLBACK
[check] enrolled in session 29763: 3/6
RESULT: B waited for A, then the UNIQUE constraint rejected the duplicate booking.

==============================================================================
PART 3 — FIX 1: enrol_swimmer() takes SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the session row
==============================================================================
[reset] session 29763 now has 5/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] SELECT enrol_swimmer(29763, 17, 'Card')
[A]   -> (60033,)
[B] BEGIN
[B]   -> BEGIN
[B] SELECT enrol_swimmer(29763, 18, 'Card')
[B]   ... BLOCKED (pg_stat_activity.wait_event_type = Lock)
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
[B]   -> ERROR:  Session 29763 is full (capacity=6, already enrolled=6)   (SQLSTATE 23514)
[B] ROLLBACK
[B]   -> ROLLBACK
[check] enrolled in session 29763: 6/6
RESULT: B waited on A's row lock, re-counted after A committed and was refused — 6/6, no overbooking.

==============================================================================
PART 4 — FIX 2: SERIALIZABLE isolation (no explicit lock), with one retry
==============================================================================
[reset] session 29763 now has 5/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (5,)
[B] BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE
[B]   -> BEGIN
[B] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[B]   -> (5,)
[A] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[A]   -> INSERT 0 1
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 18, 'confirmed')
[B]   -> INSERT 0 1
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
[B] COMMIT
[B]   -> ERROR:  could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions   (SQLSTATE 40001)
B was ABORTED with serialization_failure (40001); the app retries B once:
[B] BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE
[B]   -> BEGIN
[B] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[B]   -> (6,)
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 18, 'confirmed')
[B]   -> ERROR:  Group session 29763 is full (capacity=6, already enrolled=6)   (SQLSTATE 23514)
[B] ROLLBACK
[B]   -> ROLLBACK
[check] enrolled in session 29763: 6/6
RESULT: 6/6 — the retry saw the lesson was full (trigger refused it), no overbooking.

==============================================================================
PART 5 — READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ (price change + new booking during A's transaction)
==============================================================================
[reset] session 29763 now has 3/6 confirmed swimmers

--- A runs at READ COMMITTED ---
[reset] session 29763 now has 3/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] SELECT price FROM lesson_session WHERE session_id = 29763
[A]   -> (Decimal('5000.00'),)
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (3,)
[B] UPDATE lesson_session SET price = 5500 WHERE session_id = 29763
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[B]   -> INSERT 0 1
(B auto-committed both changes)
[A] SELECT price FROM lesson_session WHERE session_id = 29763
[A]   -> (Decimal('5500.00'),)
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (4,)
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
A's second read: price 5500.00, enrolled 4 -> changed inside one transaction (non-repeatable read + phantom)

--- A runs at REPEATABLE READ ---
[reset] session 29763 now has 3/6 confirmed swimmers
[A] BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ
[A]   -> BEGIN
[A] SELECT price FROM lesson_session WHERE session_id = 29763
[A]   -> (Decimal('5000.00'),)
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (3,)
[B] UPDATE lesson_session SET price = 5500 WHERE session_id = 29763
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] INSERT INTO enrolment (session_id, swimmer_id, status) VALUES (29763, 17, 'confirmed')
[B]   -> INSERT 0 1
(B auto-committed both changes)
[A] SELECT price FROM lesson_session WHERE session_id = 29763
[A]   -> (Decimal('5000.00'),)
[A] SELECT count(*) FROM enrolment WHERE session_id = 29763 AND status IN ('confirmed','attended')
[A]   -> (3,)
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
A's second read: price 5000.00, enrolled 3 -> same snapshot as the first read (no non-repeatable read, no phantom)

==============================================================================
PART 6 — DEADLOCK: A and B update two swimmers in OPPOSITE order
==============================================================================
[A] BEGIN
[A]   -> BEGIN
[B] BEGIN
[B]   -> BEGIN
[A] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 19
[A]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 24
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[A] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 24
[A]   ... BLOCKED (pg_stat_activity.wait_event_type = Lock)
[B] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 19
[B]   ... BLOCKED (pg_stat_activity.wait_event_type = Lock)
[A]   -> ERROR:  deadlock detected   (SQLSTATE 40P01)
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[A] ROLLBACK
[A]   -> ROLLBACK
[B] COMMIT
[B]   -> COMMIT
RESULT: PostgreSQL found the cycle (deadlock_timeout = 1s) and aborted one transaction (40P01).

--- The fix: both sessions lock in the SAME order (lowest swimmer_id first) ---
[A] BEGIN
[A]   -> BEGIN
[B] BEGIN
[B]   -> BEGIN
[A] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 19
[A]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 19
[B]   ... BLOCKED (pg_stat_activity.wait_event_type = Lock)
[A] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 24
[A]   -> UPDATE 1
[A] COMMIT
[A]   -> COMMIT
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] UPDATE swimmer SET phone = phone WHERE swimmer_id = 24
[B]   -> UPDATE 1
[B] COMMIT
[B]   -> COMMIT
RESULT: B simply waited for A, then finished. Same lock order = no deadlock.

